[{:db/id 17592186134214 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}6x\\,dy]dA,\n$$\n\nEvaluate the integral above over the right bounded region $D$ in the $xz$-plane: $0\\le x\\le2$ and $0\\le z\\le4-x^2$. Its boundary consists of $z=0$, $x=0$, and $z+x^2=4$, on the nonnegative-$x$ side." :question/worked-solution "The clarified region is the right bounded lobe, $D=\\{(x,z):0\\le x\\le2,0\\le z\\le4-x^2\\}$. Integrate first with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\\int_{x^2}^4 6x\\,dy=6x(4-x^2).$$\n\nThe $z$ interval has length $4-x^2$, so\n\n$$\\iint_D\\left[\\int_{x^2}^4 6x\\,dy\\right]dA=\\int_0^2 6x(4-x^2)^2\\,dx=[-(4-x^2)^3]_0^2=64.$$\n\nAll inner limits are ordered. The original boundary description also encloses a left lobe, $-2\\le x\\le0$, whose integral is $-64$; it did not explicitly select a side of $x=0$. Selecting the right lobe explicitly gives $64$." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
